## 15. Real APK build pipeline

The generated Flutter source must be compiled by a separate build worker.

The backend must submit an authenticated job containing an immutable source snapshot or securely referenced archive.

The worker must:
1. Create an isolated, resource-limited workspace.
2. Validate and safely extract the project.
3. Verify the expected Flutter files.
4. Resolve dependencies.
5. Run formatting, analysis and configured tests.
6. Run the actual Flutter APK build command.
7. Check process exit codes.
8. Confirm the APK exists and is nonempty.
9. Validate available artifact metadata and calculate a checksum.
10. Upload the APK to protected storage.
11. Clean up temporary files.
12. Return the actual result and sanitized logs.

Use a clear job lifecycle: queued, preparing, building, validating, succeeded or failed.

Do not run untrusted generated code with unrestricted access to the backend or host machine.

Enforce build timeouts, resource limits, storage limits, authorization checks and restricted network access.

Provide a documented local worker setup or a separately hosted build-worker configuration when the current platform cannot compile APKs.

The Android app must display a genuine build status and offer a download only after a real APK artifact has been validated.

Distinguish debug APKs from signed release artifacts. Protect signing credentials if release signing is supported.

